# Q11 — Do SSE events for an MCP tool call carry the session id + tool output?
Date: 2026-10-13. Host Bun 1.4.2 MCP server (/tmp/opencode/q14/mcp.ts,
spike_echo tool, port 8377). Container agency-spike-q11 (agency-base:2.0.26,
bridge, --add-host=host.docker.internal:host-gateway, SPIKE_MCP_ENABLE=1,
spike/config). Model: opencode/cat-jam-free. SSE: background
`curl -N /api/event` (Basic auth) captured to a file during each run.

## Commands + trimmed output

$ curl -X POST /api/experimental/mcp/bunprobe/connect   # first: 404 (lazy registration)
$ curl -X POST /api/experimental/mcp/bunprobe/connect   # retry: 204
$ curl /api/mcp
{"data":[{"name":"bunprobe","status":{"status":"connected"}},
         {"name":"spikeprobe","status":{"status":"disabled"}}]}

$ # attempt 1: session WITHOUT any MCP permission allow, prompt names spike_echo
$ curl -X POST /api/session {...,"permissions":[]...} -> ses_ec8e9dce8ffeZvD1lA0wCJmBft
assistant tool part (trimmed):
{"type":"tool","name":"spike_echo","executed":false,
 "state":{"status":"error","input":{"text":"hello-sse"},
  "error":{"type":"tool.execution",
   "message":"No tool named \"spike_echo\" is currently available. ..."}}}
# -> without a per-server allow, the MCP tool is permission-filtered out of
# the model's tool list entirely (same for name bunprobe_spike_echo).
# Tool naming to the model is <server>_<tool>; the grant must be
# {"action":"bunprobe_*","resource":"*","effect":"allow"} — exactly the
# per-server allow shared context 9.6 requires Stage 1c to pass per task.

$ # attempt 2: session WITH the MCP allow, prompt names bunprobe_spike_echo
$ curl -X POST /api/session {"title":"q11c","agent":"spike",
  "model":{"providerID":"opencode","id":"cat-jam-free"},"location":{...},
  "permissions":[{"action":"bunprobe_*","resource":"*","effect":"allow"}]}
{"data":{"id":"ses_ec8e3a650ffe35djgRiTk1WjQE",...}}          # HTTP 200
$ curl .../prompt {"text":"Call the tool bunprobe_spike_echo once ..."} -> 200
$ curl -X POST /api/experimental/session/<id>/wait -> 204
$ curl /api/session/<id>/message  (trimmed)
assistant tool part:
{"type":"tool","name":"bunprobe_spike_echo","executed":false,
 "state":{"status":"completed","input":{"text":"hello-sse3"},
  "content":[{"type":"text","text":"echo:hello-sse3"}]}}
assistant text: "echo:hello-sse3 ... ## RESULT ```json {...} ```"
idle: succeeded

$ # SSE frames from the background capture during attempt 2 (30 data frames):
session.tool.called:
{"id":"evt_1371c6ad0001RG2jXXIVglHG7g","created":1791925971664,
 "type":"session.tool.called",
 "location":{"directory":"/tmp/tmp.7OH8VOWwVZ/trees/t1"},
 "data":{"sessionID":"ses_ec8e3a650ffe35djgRiTk1WjQE",
  "assistantMessageID":"msg_1371c6679001MDkEPPMYuAKknQ",
  "id":"call-adb3c86b-eea8-485c-8a08-ad83830c95bc",
  "input":{"text":"hello-sse3"},"executed":false},...}
session.tool.success:
{"id":"evt_1371c6ae30013TTyme86jptqiB","created":1791925971683,
 "type":"session.tool.success",
 "location":{"directory":"/tmp/tmp.7OH8VOWwVZ/trees/t1"},
 "data":{"sessionID":"ses_ec8e3a650ffe35djgRiTk1WjQE",
  "assistantMessageID":"msg_1371c6679001MDkEPPMYuAKknQ",
  "id":"call-adb3c86b-eea8-485c-8a08-ad83830c95bc",
  "content":[{"type":"text","text":"echo:hello-sse3"}],
  "metadata":{"truncated":false},"executed":false},...}
# -> both frames carry the session id; called carries the call id + input,
# success carries the same call id + the tool output text. The Dispatcher
# can match a fleet_mission_create call to its session by sessionID/call id.

Anomaly (recorded, non-blocking): the FIRST q11 container became
API-unresponsive (0% CPU, /api/info timeout) after repeated client-side
60-150 s timeout POSTs to the connect endpoint; a fresh container with the
same config/image connected at once (404 then 204 in ms). Unexplained;
kept the wedged container's log line count (3 boot lines) as the observation.

Hygiene note: the unconstrained attempt-1/2 sessions show the model dumps
container env (including OPENCODE_API_KEY/OPENCODE_PASSWORD-derived strings)
into transcripts via shell. This log is hand-transcribed with all secrets
redacted; raw transcripts lived only in ephemeral container DBs, destroyed
with the containers (`docker rm -f`). Stage 1b/1c must redact transcripts
before storing evidence, and experts should not see raw provider secrets.

## Observation
Yes. session.tool.called and session.tool.success each carry sessionID
(plus assistantMessageID and the shared call id); success carries the tool
output content. Matching needs no polling.

## Verdict: yes (Director-polls fallback not needed)
